#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""haiku-triage-failures -- sort the DeBeOS build-wave failure backlog against the
arm64 porting playbook (graviton/docs/porting-playbook.md, issue #90 / #58).

WHY THIS EXISTS (issue #136 + #90). The campaign accumulates a large, opaque
backlog -- as of this writing 572 `failed` + ~180 `needs_human` items in the
`debeos-package-state` DDB table. "572 failed" is not an honest status: the
playbook's 14 failure classes say most of that number is *known* quick-win recipe
work (CMake policy floor, config.guess arm64, gettext autopoint, x86 SIMD guards,
static-lib install, undeclared build tools), while a genuinely-hard residue needs
real source porting, a *published* dependency, or a browser-solved fetch. This
script is the committed, re-runnable classifier that replaces the flat count with
a sorted census: N auto-recoverable across which classes, M genuinely-hard, K
unmatched/no-log.

WHAT IT IS. Deterministic triage glue, nothing more. It does NOT rebuild, re-wave,
or re-queue anything -- classification only. It:
  1. enumerates every item with build_state in {failed, needs_human} off the
     `by-build-state` GSI (same index the wave driver reads);
  2. fetches that port's most-recent build log from S3 -- the `log_url` attribute
     RecordOutcome persisted (s3://<work-bucket>/logs/<run>/<pkg>.log.gz, gzipped);
  3. classifies the log against the playbook's class signatures using an
     ORDERED, first-match-wins ruleset (most-specific signature first) -- the
     Quick-index phrase->class table from the playbook, encoded as RULES below;
  4. emits per port: triage_class (a class number | NO_LOG | UNMATCHED), triage_auto_recoverable
     (bool), triage_fix_ref (playbook section anchor), triage_ts;
  5. (only with --execute) writes those four NEW attributes back to the item --
     ADDITIVE ONLY, it never touches build_state or any existing attribute;
  6. prints a census summary: count per class, auto-recoverable vs genuinely-hard
     vs UNMATCHED/NO_LOG.

auto_recoverable HONESTY (the point of the exercise -- see the playbook's #47
caveat). A class is auto_recoverable=True only when the playbook says a systemic
env/recipe change with a known template clears it (classes 1-11, 13, 14 below).
It is False where "recoverable in principle" hides a per-port judgement call:
  * Class 12 (source drift) -- a checksum bump is only safe *after* the integrity
    gate (fetch, sha256, extract, verify version), and the egress-blocked subset
    (Cloudflare challenge / unreachable port) is "Not fixable by recipe" at all.
    So class 12 is False with a note: it needs per-port work, not a blind re-wave.
Never silently bucket an UNMATCHED log as recoverable: UNMATCHED and NO_LOG are
always counted as not-auto-recoverable, and UNMATCHED logs are dumped (with
--dump-unmatched) so the ruleset can be extended rather than guessed at.

UNMATCHED IS TWO THINGS, AND THE CENSUS SAYS WHICH (#488). A log can go unclassified
because the ruleset has no signature for it, or because the captured window contains no
diagnostic line at all -- the failure path used `tail -20 "$log"` before 3371e78289
(2026-09-17), and ~11 of those ~22 lines are haikuporter's fixed epilogue, so for most
of the pre-fix backlog the window ends before the line that says WHY. The second kind is
unclassifiable by construction: no regex can read a line that was never captured. Those
are reported on their own census line (NO_DIAGNOSTIC), carried per port as
`no_diagnostic_captured` in the JSON/CSV, and dumped into --dump-unmatched's
`no-diagnostic/` subdirectory. They need a re-wave with the current deep capture, NOT a
new signature -- and they must not be counted against classifier coverage. Measured over
the 639-log backlog cache: 129 UNMATCHED, of which 105 have no diagnostic and only 24
are a genuine ruleset gap (each a distinct per-port error).

SAFETY / SOP ALIGNMENT (graviton/ops/SOPs.md):
  * DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT. With no --execute it reads DDB + S3 (describe/get only),
    classifies, prints the census, and writes NOTHING back. The DDB writes are the
    only mutation, are gated behind --execute, and are additive attributes only
    (triage_*). Its blast radius matches a read-only role plus a scoped UpdateItem.
  * Re-runnable: it re-reads live state each run and overwrites only its own
    triage_* attributes, so re-running after more ports fail (or after a ruleset
    tweak) just refreshes the classification. It is idempotent for a fixed backlog
    + ruleset.

USAGE
  # classify the whole backlog, print the census, write nothing (safe):
  AWS_PROFILE=haiku-graviton graviton/scripts/haiku-triage-failures

  # same, but also persist triage_* back to each item:
  AWS_PROFILE=haiku-graviton graviton/scripts/haiku-triage-failures --execute

  # smoke a small sample and see which phrase matched each:
  ... --limit 20 --show-matches

  # write the full per-port classification to a JSON/CSV artifact:
  ... --out /tmp/triage.json --csv /tmp/triage.csv

  # inspect what did NOT match any class (to extend the ruleset):
  ... --dump-unmatched /tmp/unmatched

Exit status: 0 on a clean classification (dry-run or --execute); non-zero on a
usage/credentials error or if the DDB scan / S3 access could not be performed.
"""
import argparse
import concurrent.futures as cf
import csv
import gzip
import io
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
from collections import Counter, defaultdict

TABLE_DEFAULT = os.environ.get("TABLE", "debeos-package-state")
GSI = "by-build-state"
STATES = ("failed", "needs_human")
PLAYBOOK = "graviton/docs/porting-playbook.md"


def log(*a):
    print(*a, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


def anchor(slug):
    return "%s#%s" % (PLAYBOOK, slug)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- the ruleset
# ORDERED, first-match-wins. Most-specific / least-ambiguous signatures first so a
# generic phrase (Class 2's "command not found", Class 11's "cannot stat") never
# shadows a specific one (Class 13's cross-prefix 127, Class 3's autopoint 127).
# Each rule: (class_no, slug, auto_recoverable, [compiled regexes], note).
# `patterns` are matched case-sensitively against the full decompressed log.
def _rx(*pats):
    return [re.compile(p) for p in pats]


RULES = [
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- FIRST BLOCK
    # Classes 15, 30, 31, 34 (#488): OUR OWN TOOLING'S REFUSALS. haikuporter (or the
    # wave driver) declined *before* or *instead of* running a compiler, so the window
    # holds no build output that could be a better cause -- and every signature here is
    # an exact literal from our own tooling, not from a third-party build system. They
    # go first for a second, less obvious reason: a log that aborts in the *pre*-build
    # steps still carries the chatter of the autoreconf/automake run that SUCCEEDED
    # ("+ Running automake: configure.ac:358: installing './compile'"), and that chatter
    # is one reword away from tripping class 3 or class 10. Ordering them ahead of the
    # autotools classes makes that impossible instead of merely unlikely.
    # BE HONEST ABOUT THAT SECOND REASON: it is a GUARD, not a fix for an observed
    # misfiling. Measured -- moving class 15 to after classes 3 and 10 reclassifies
    # nothing in the 639-log cache, because no runConfigure log there matches either. The
    # selftest therefore pins it with a synthetic fixture (the guard line plus a class-10
    # line in one window); without that fixture the position would be an untested claim.

    # 15 -- runConfigure refuses a CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS override with no -O flag. The single
    # largest unclassified shape in the #488 census (20 ports). NOTE the spelling: the
    # guard fires for BOTH CFLAGS and CXXFLAGS, and two of the 20 (farmhash, libnpupnp)
    # only ever log the CXXFLAGS wording -- a CFLAGS-only regex silently misses them,
    # which is why the character class is there.
    (15, "class-15-runconfigure-cflags-override-without-optimization-flags", True,
     _rx(r"runConfigure: Must specify optimization flags when overriding C(?:XX)?FLAGS"),
     "Add -O2 (or -O3) to the recipe's CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS override -- runConfigure "
     "deliberately refuses to fall back to the default optimization flags when a recipe "
     "overrides the variable, so the override must carry its own -O."),

    # 34 -- haikuporter's meson analogue of class 8's cmake guard. NOT folded into class
    # 8: the fix is a different flag in a different build system, and filing it under a
    # class named "missing CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE" would send the reader to the wrong recipe
    # line. Same shape, different route -> its own class.
    (34, "class-34-meson-invoked-without---buildtype", True,
     _rx(r"invoking meson without --buildtype"),
     "Pass --buildtype=release (or debugoptimized) in the recipe's meson invocation, "
     "the meson analogue of class 8's -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release."),

    # 30 -- the recipe names a patch file that is not staged in the overlay. NOT a port
    # defect and NOT arm64-specific: an OPERATOR/overlay-staging bug (a patchset that
    # never got copied in, or a PATCHES= entry with a typo'd name -- criticalmass names
    # `...patchet`, veesem names a `.recipe`). Its own class precisely because it routes
    # somewhere different from every other class: to whoever stages the overlay, not to
    # the port. auto=False -- there is no template, the missing file has to be found.
    (30, "class-30-recipe-names-a-patch-file-that-is-not-staged", False,
     _rx(r'Error: patch file "[^"]+" not found'),
     "OPERATOR, not the port: stage the missing patch file into the overlay (or fix the "
     "PATCHES= entry's filename). Check the overlay-sync step before re-waving."),

    # 31 -- the wave asked for a port that has no recipe anywhere in the tree. Also an
    # operator/wave-list issue rather than a build failure: haikuporter aborts at lookup.
    (31, "class-31-port-has-no-recipe-in-the-tree", False,
     _rx(r"Error: \S+ not found in repository"),
     "OPERATOR, not the port: the wave list names a port with no recipe in the overlay "
     "or upstream tree. Write the recipe, or drop the name from the wave list."),

    # 14 -- poisoned build-tool package. Unmistakable magic constant.
    (14, "class-14-poisoned-build-tool-package", True,
     _rx(r"Unhandled pheader type in parse 0x6474e553"),
     "Quarantine the poisoned bison_bootstrap hpkg out of the pool (one-time "
     "operator/pool action, not a recipe edit); then the class clears pool-wide."),

    # 6 -- config.guess cannot name arm64. Specific autotools/CMake host-triple abort.
    (6, "class-6-configguess-cannot-name-the-arm64-host", True,
     _rx(r"cannot guess build type",
         r"GetHostTriple\.cmake",
         r"Failed to execute.*config\.guess"),
     "Route autotools through runConfigure, or replace the raw-read config.guess "
     "(LLVM-style) with an arm64->aarch64 stub. #71."),

    # 19 -- build system does not recognise aarch64/arm64. The PROJECT'S OWN arch
    # switch (not GNU config.guess -- that is Class 6, matched just above). "Cannot
    # guess HOST type" (vs Class 6's "guess BUILD type"), an empty/unknown $(ARCH)
    # aborting a Makefile/Make.inc, or a bespoke "unsupported platform" bail.
    # auto=False: the fix is a per-port source patch to the project's arch switch
    # and several are deferred-hard (julia/ocaml/jamvm codegen) -- not a template.
    (19, "class-19-build-system-does-not-recognise-aarch64arm64", False,
     _rx(r"Cannot guess host type",
         r'Platform \'\' not supported',
         r"unknown word-size for arch",
         r"Your platform is unsupported",
         r"Architecture not detected"),
     "Patch the project's own arch/platform switch to add an arm64/aarch64 branch "
     "(map to the 64-bit LE codegen path); per-port, some deferred-hard."),

    # 3 -- gettext autoreconf / autopoint. Must precede Class 10 (autoreconf) and
    # Class 2 (missing tool), both of which a po/-tree failure also trips.
    (3, "class-3-gettext-autoreconf--autopoint", True,
     _rx(r"AM_GNU_GETTEXT_VERSION",
         r"AM_GNU_GETTEXT\b",
         r'Can\'t exec "autopoint"',
         r"autopoint failed",
         r"Internationalization tools missing",
         # gettext/NLS m4 macros not on the aclocal path surface as "possibly
         # undefined macro: AM_NLS / AM_GNU_GETTEXT / AM_ICONV ..." + AC_LIB_*.
         r"possibly undefined macro: (?:AM_NLS|AM_GNU_GETTEXT|AM_ICONV|AM_GLIB_GNU_GETTEXT)",
         r"possibly undefined macro: AC_LIB_",
         # same mechanism (a provider's m4 macro off the aclocal path), different
         # provider: libxml2's AM_PATH_XML2 (libmetalink/libspectrum). Fix is the
         # same ACLOCAL_PATH + autoreconf refresh.
         r"possibly undefined macro: AM_PATH_XML2"),
     "Declare cmd:autopoint and put gettext's m4 dir on ACLOCAL_PATH; "
     "autopoint --force before autoreconf -fi."),

    # 10 -- missing autotools aux files. Specific configure phrase. The aux file is
    # NOT always install-sh: libmcrypt aborts on 'ltmain.sh', libsrtp on 'ar-lib'
    # (both `configure.in/.ac: … required file 'X' not found`), so key on the
    # generic "required file 'X' not found" shape, not the install-sh literal.
    (10, "class-10-missing-autotools-aux-files", True,
     _rx(r"required file '?install-sh'? not found",
         r"required file '[^']+' not found",
         r"try running autoreconf --install",
         r"run autoreconf --install"),
     "Use autoreconf -fi (force + install aux files)."),

    # 29 -- the autotools re-run itself breaks (#488): aclocal/autom4te/autoreconf/
    # automake abort, typically because the recipe's own autoreconf hits a macro the
    # project invokes twice (fswatch: AC_CONFIG_MACRO_DIR used once only; irrxml:
    # AM_INIT_AUTOMAKE expanded multiple times) under a newer autoconf/automake than the
    # source was written for. ORDERED AFTER CLASS 10 ON PURPOSE: an autoreconf that
    # aborts *because an aux file is missing* prints BOTH its own "automake failed with
    # exit status: 1" and class 10's "required file '...' not found" -- libmcrypt is
    # exactly that log, and class 10 names the cause while this class names only the
    # messenger. Putting 29 first would re-file libmcrypt under a class whose fix
    # (patch the macro) does not apply to it. Measured: 3 logs match this signature,
    # 1 of them (libmcrypt) is correctly kept by class 10.
    (29, "class-29-the-autotools-re-run-itself-breaks", True,
     _rx(r"AC_CONFIG_MACRO_DIR can only be used once",
         r"AM_INIT_AUTOMAKE expanded multiple times",
         r"(?:aclocal|autom4te|autoreconf|automake): error: .* failed with exit status"),
     "Patch the duplicated autoconf/automake macro invocation out of configure.ac "
     "(AC_CONFIG_MACRO_DIR / AM_INIT_AUTOMAKE once only), or pin the recipe to the "
     "autotools version the source expects. Not arm64-specific."),

    # 1 -- CMake 4.x pre-3.5 policy removal. The largest class.
    (1, "class-1-cmake-4x-pre-35-policy-removal", True,
     _rx(r"Compatibility with CMake < 3\.5 has been removed",
         r"CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM"),
     "Add -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5. #47 caveat: the systemic haikuporter "
     "patch only reaches recipes that interpolate $cmakeDirArgs; recipes with an "
     "explicit cmake arg list (most #280 ports) still need the per-recipe -D."),

    # 8 -- missing / bad CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE.
    (8, "class-8-missing-cmake_build_type", True,
     _rx(r"invoking cmake without CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE specified",
         r"without debug info packages specified"),
     "Set -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release (usually alongside the Class 1 policy flag)."),

    # 17 -- CMake FetchContent/ExternalProject wants the network at configure time. The
    # third class the playbook reserved in #309 and never encoded (betterspades,
    # grcompiler, openexr3.2). Distinct from class 12: that is the recipe's own
    # SOURCE_URI, this is a build-time sub-dependency the upstream CMakeLists pulls
    # itself, which the sandboxed chroot cannot reach. auto=False -- pre-seeding a
    # sub-dependency is per-port work, not a flag.
    # ORDERED AFTER CLASS 1: when the *fetched* subproject then trips the pre-3.5 policy
    # removal, class 1's -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM is the fix and must win. The
    # `Modules/FetchContent.cmake:<line>` anchor is what all three logs share -- cmake
    # prints that frame only when it is unwinding an error through FetchContent.
    (17, "class-17-cmake-fetchcontent-wants-the-network-at-configure-time", False,
     _rx(r"Modules/FetchContent\.cmake:\d+",
         r"FetchContent_MakeAvailable",
         r"__FetchContent_populateSubbuild"),
     "Supply the sub-dependency as a real package and point "
     "-DFETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_<name> at it (or vendor it into the recipe). NOT class "
     "12: the failing fetch is the upstream CMakeLists', not the recipe's SOURCE_URI."),

    # 5 -- x86-only compiler flags on arm64.
    (5, "class-5-x86-only-compiler-flags-on-arm64", True,
     _rx(r"unrecognized command-line option '-msse\d?'",
         r"unrecognized command-line option .-msse",
         # other x86-only -m flags aarch64 gcc rejects the same way: -mmmx
         # (opendune), -m3dnow, -mavx*. Same gate on x86/x86_64 as -msse.
         r"unrecognized command-line option '-m(?:mmx|3dnow|avx\w*|fpmath=sse)'",
         # an x86 MICROARCHITECTURE passed to -march/-mtune/-mcpu (waveedit:
         # `-march=nocona`). Deliberately spelled as an explicit list of x86 core names
         # rather than the generic `unknown value '.*' for '-march'` -- the generic form
         # would also claim a bad *arm* value (a mistyped Graviton3/4 -mcpu=neoverse-*,
         # see the playbook's ISA opt-in section), which is a different bug with a
         # different fix. Found while verifying #488; not in that issue's table.
         r"unknown value '(?:nocona|core2|corei7\w*|k8|athlon\w*|opteron\w*|barcelona|"
         r"pentium\w*|prescott|i[3-6]86|x86-64\w*|atom|nehalem|westmere|sandybridge|"
         r"ivybridge|haswell|broadwell|skylake\w*|znver\d)' for '-m(?:arch|tune|cpu)'",
         r"-mfpmath=sse",
         r"error:.*'-m(?:sse|mmx|3dnow|avx)",
         # #488: the same bug arriving through the x86 intrinsic HEADER rather than the
         # -m flag. aarch64 gcc accepts -DUSE_MMX happily and then the source does
         # `#include <mmintrin.h>`, which only exists on x86. Note `[a-z]*mmintrin`
         # (star, not a single letter): the bare spelling `mmintrin.h` is the one
         # sdl_gfx hits, and a `[a-z]mmintrin` character class silently misses it --
         # measured, that typo scored this signature 0 hits. Best leverage on the #488
         # list: one port, but sdl_gfx gates 9 downstream ports.
         r"(?:[a-z]*mmintrin|x86intrin|cpuid)\.h: No such file or directory"),
     "Gate -msse*/-mmmx/-m3dnow/-mavx/-mfpmath=sse on x86/x86_64 via "
     "$effectiveTargetArchitecture (CMake) or $targetArchitecture (Makefile sed). Same "
     "gate for an x86 -march/-mtune/-mcpu value (-march=nocona) and for the x86 "
     "intrinsic HEADERS (<mmintrin.h>/<*mmintrin.h>/<x86intrin.h>/<cpuid.h>): drop "
     "the -DUSE_MMX-style define on arm64 rather than the feature."),

    # 21 -- python build-time module missing from the chroot. The playbook titled
    # this setuptools/pkg_resources, but the real backlog is broader and the same
    # shape: a build/setup.py imports a python module not in the chroot --
    # pkg_resources/setuptools (html5lib, pbr), distutils (librnp; removed in 3.12,
    # provided by setuptools), or jinja2 (libplacebo; a real codegen dep). Fix is
    # always "declare the providing python package in BUILD_PREREQUIRES".
    # #488 folds the "Cannot find Python 3 development headers" shape in here rather
    # than giving it its own class: one port (libsigrokdecode), and the route is the
    # same -- add the missing python build requirement to the recipe. Only the spelling
    # of the requirement differs (a devel:/_devel headers subpackage instead of a
    # python module), which the note below states.
    (21, "class-21-python-setuppy-needs-setuptools--pkg_resources", True,
     _rx(r"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '[^']+'",
         r"import pkg_resources\b.*ModuleNotFoundError",
         r"Cannot find Python \d[^ ]* development headers"),
     "Declare the providing python package in BUILD_PREREQUIRES (setuptools "
     "supplies pkg_resources/distutils; jinja2 etc. are their own package), "
     "matching the recipe's python version. For 'Cannot find Python N development "
     "headers' the missing requirement is the python _devel/headers subpackage in "
     "BUILD_REQUIRES, not a module -- same route, different spelling."),

    # 23 -- install writes to read-only /packages. Specific packagefs write failure
    # in the harvest step (binary already built). auto=False: needs a per-port look
    # at the subpackage identifier (often a stray-whitespace PROVIDES/version bug).
    (23, "class-23-install-writes-to-read-only-packages", False,
     _rx(r"cannot create directory '/packages/[^']*': Read-only file system",
         r"/packages/[^ ]*: Read-only file system"),
     "Correct the subpackage identifier (strip stray whitespace in PROVIDES/"
     "version); ensure the split INSTALL() writes under $developInstallDir/$prefix, "
     "not an absolute /packages path."),

    # 24 -- source-acquisition tool missing on the builder HOST (hg/svn/bzr/lha).
    # Fetch/unpack runs on the host before the chroot exists, so a cmd: prereq can't
    # help. auto=True: systemic provisioner host-tool add clears it on a rebake.
    (24, "class-24-source-acquisition-tool-missing-on-the-builder-host", True,
     _rx(r"'(?:hg|svn|bzr|cvs|lha|git)' is not available, please install it",
         r"is not available, please install it"),
     "Add the VCS/archive client (hg/svn/bzr/lha) to the host install list in "
     "haiku-provision-native-builder -- NOT a recipe cmd: prereq."),

    # 18 -- missing link-time library (ld: cannot find -l<lib>). A Haiku system
    # library whose unversioned devel symlink is absent (libmidi/libmidi2/
    # libscreensaver) or a third-party lib not in the pool (that subset is really
    # Class 20). auto=True: the Haiku-lib majority clears via a systemic
    # devel-symlink overlay + per-recipe devel: declaration.
    (18, "class-18-missing-link-time-library-ld-cannot-find--llib", True,
     _rx(r"ld: cannot find -l\S+: No such file or directory",
         r"cannot find -l\S+: No such file or directory",
         # same failure, spelled as an absolute path because the link line names the
         # .so directly instead of passing -l (textsaver:
         # `ld: cannot find /boot/system/lib/libscreensaver.so`). libscreensaver is one
         # of the very devel symlinks this class's note is about, so missing it here was
         # a pure spelling gap. Anchored on /boot/system/lib|/packages so an arbitrary
         # relative object file cannot claim it. Found while verifying #488.
         r"ld: cannot find (?:/boot/system/lib|/packages)/\S+\.so[^:]*: "
         r"No such file or directory"),
     "Declare the providing devel:/lib: build requirement, or add the missing "
     "Haiku devel symlink (systemic overlay). Never drop the -l (cuts the feature)."),

    # 16 -- duplicate symbols at link time because gcc >= 10 defaults to -fno-common
    # (#488, 13 ports). NOT an arm64 bug and NOT a Haiku bug: pre-C99 sources declare a
    # tentative definition in a header, every TU that includes it emits its own, and
    # -fcommon used to merge them. The fix is arch-independent and the class would look
    # identical on x86.
    # ORDERED IMMEDIATELY AFTER CLASS 18, the other ld class, and deliberately BEFORE
    # class 33 (the generic `undefined reference to`): among link failures the one that
    # names *which* symbol and *why* wins, and a -fno-common link can emit undefined
    # references as collateral. It sits after 18 because a link whose *inputs* are
    # missing (`cannot find -lfoo`) has not got as far as comparing symbols -- fix the
    # input first. Measured: none of the 13 logs also carries `cannot find -l` or
    # `undefined reference`, so the order is a guard for future logs, not a live tie.
    (16, "class-16--fno-common-duplicate-symbols-at-link-time", True,
     _rx(r"multiple definition of `[^']+'"),
     "Add -fcommon to the recipe's CFLAGS (gcc >= 10 flipped the default to "
     "-fno-common), or -- better where the source is short -- patch the tentative "
     "definitions in the header to `extern` plus one real definition in a .c file. "
     "Arch-independent: this is a compiler-default change, not an arm64 port bug."),

    # 7 -- LTO without a linker plugin.
    (7, "class-7-lto-without-a-linker-plugin", True,
     _rx(r"LTO support has not been enabled",
         r"only with linker plugin",
         r"-fno-fat-lto-objects"),
     "Drop LTO: turn off CMAKE_INTERPROCEDURAL_OPTIMIZATION or sed -flto/"
     "-fuse-linker-plugin out of the Makefile."),

    # 13 -- hardcoded cross-compiler prefix. A specific 127; must precede Class 2.
    (13, "class-13-hardcoded-cross-compiler-prefix", True,
     _rx(r"aarch64-unknown-haiku-(?:g\+\+|gcc|c\+\+|cc|ld|ar)\b.*"
         r"(?:No such file or directory|command not found|Error 127)",
         r"aarch64-unknown-haiku-.*No such file or directory"),
     "Pass native CXX=g++/CC=gcc. Class-wide cleared by #186 (arm64 triplet "
     "compilers on the in-chroot PATH)."),

    # 27 -- C++11 made a narrowing conversion in a braced initialiser ill-formed (#488).
    # NOTE THE `error:` PREFIX, which the issue's proposed regex left out and which
    # costs this class 2 of its 5 claimed ports. -Wnarrowing is emitted as a WARNING far
    # more often than as an error (qemacs and colors each show only warnings, and in
    # colors' case in a different translation unit from the object that actually failed),
    # so a regex that accepts the warning form files logs by a line that did not fail the
    # build. Those two stay UNMATCHED, which is the honest answer: their real diagnostic
    # is off the end of the truncated window (see the NO_DIAGNOSTIC bucket).
    # Placed BEFORE class 22: `error: narrowing conversion of ... [-Wnarrowing]` is
    # strictly more specific than class 22's `does not name a type`, and it carries a
    # template fix where class 22 means per-port source porting.
    (27, "class-27-c11-narrowing-conversion-is-an-error", True,
     _rx(r"error: narrowing conversion of .* \[-Wnarrowing\]"),
     "Cast at the initialiser (the correct fix), or -- as a class-wide unblock -- add "
     "-Wno-narrowing / -fpermissive to the recipe's CXXFLAGS. Arch-independent: a C++11 "
     "conformance tightening, not an arm64 port bug."),

    # 32 -- Linux-only CPU-affinity / cpuset API (#488). glibc's sched_setaffinity
    # helpers (CPU_ALLOC, CPU_ISSET_S, CPU_*_S) have no Haiku equivalent, so the port
    # needs real work: either an affinity-free code path or a shim. Note cpuid, whose
    # own patchset already tried a shim (`#define CPU_ZERO CPUSET_ZERO`) against a name
    # Haiku does not export -- so it surfaces at LINK time as
    # `undefined reference to `CPUSET_ZERO''. That is why this class must precede class
    # 33: 33 would otherwise claim cpuid and file a named, understood porting gap as an
    # anonymous missing symbol. The selftest asserts exactly that ordering.
    (32, "class-32-linux-only-cpu-affinity--cpuset-api", False,
     _rx(r"CPU_ISSET_S", r"CPU_ALLOC", r"CPU_ZERO_S", r"CPU_SET_S", r"CPUSET_ZERO"),
     "Real porting work: give the source an affinity-free path under __HAIKU__ (or a "
     "correct shim -- Haiku exports neither CPU_*_S nor CPUSET_ZERO). Not a template; "
     "not recoverable by a re-wave."),

    # 22 -- removed/renamed Haiku API or moved header. NARROW on purpose: only the
    # unambiguous API-rename signature "does not name a type"; broad forms (a moved
    # header, "was not declared") over-match ordinary compile errors, so they stay
    # per-port. auto=False: real source porting, several deferred-hard.
    (22, "class-22-removedrenamed-haiku-api-or-moved-header", False,
     _rx(r"does not name a type"),
     "Update the source to the current Haiku API (a PATCH() hunk) or add the moved "
     "header's include path; where the delta is large, mark deferred-hard."),

    # 4 -- static/shared lib install conflict.
    (4, "class-4-staticshared-lib-install-conflict", True,
     _rx(r"rm: cannot remove '[^']*\.(?:a|la)'",
         # the live wording is SINGULAR ("prepareInstalledDevelLib error: there is
         # both a shared and a static library"): linenoise, sdl_net, libspectrum.
         # Match both spellings with an optional trailing 's'.
         r"prepareInstalledDevelLibs?"),
     "rm -f (tolerant) the static lib, and drop it before prepareInstalledDevelLibs."),

    # 11 -- install target references optional/arch-named outputs. Fairly specific.
    (11, "class-11-install-target-references-optional-outputs", True,
     _rx(r"mv: cannot stat ",
         r"sed: can'?t read .*x86_64",
         r"lvp_icd\.x86_64\.json"),
     "Guard the optional move ([ -d ... ] && mv), or glob arch-named files "
     "(lvp_icd.*.json) instead of hardcoding the x86 spelling."),

    # 2 -- undeclared build tool in the chroot. Generic 127s: keep LATE so the
    # specific 127 classes (13) and tool-specific classes (3) win first.
    (2, "class-2-undeclared-build-tool-in-the-haikuporter-chroot", True,
     _rx(r"^\S+: command not found",
         r": command not found",
         r"\bError 127\b",
         r"msgfmt: No such file or directory",
         r"msgfmt: command not found",
         r"tar utility not found",
         r"gzip: command not found",
         r"unzip: command not found",
         r"which: command not found",
         r"msgattrib: (?:command not found|No such file)",
         # tool absence surfaced through CMake, not a bare 127: zziplib fails
         # `Could NOT find UnixCommands (missing: GZIP)`. Note this is NARROW to
         # UnixCommands -- `Could NOT find <Pkg>` for a real library (partio's
         # GLUT) is a missing dependency (Class 20), not a missing base tool.
         r"Could NOT find UnixCommands"),
     "Declare the tool as a cmd: BUILD_PREREQUIRES (see the tool->provider map). "
     "Mechanical per-recipe edit with a known template."),

    # 28 -- configure's own compile/link probe failed and the window does not say why
    # (#488). Two wordings, one routing: `C compiler cannot create executables` (the
    # chroot toolchain or its default link line is broken -- johntheripper,
    # sciteco_curses) and `could not compile test program either way` (a feature probe
    # that fails with AND without the header it is testing -- pkgconfig's bundled glib
    # on arpa/nameser_compat.h). In both cases configure prints a verdict, not a cause:
    # the cause is in config.log inside the chroot haikuporter kept for inspection. So
    # auto=False -- the action is "go read config.log", which no re-wave performs.
    # ORDERED AFTER CLASS 2, not before it: the commonest way for a C compiler to stop
    # creating executables is that the compiler or linker is not in the chroot at all, and
    # that log also carries `gcc: command not found` -- which names the cause and has a
    # template fix, where this class only says "go read config.log". Class 2 must
    # therefore win the tie. Measured: none of the three logs in this class matches class
    # 2 at all, so the order costs this class nothing; the selftest asserts the tie with a
    # synthetic fixture because no cached log exhibits it.
    (28, "class-28-configures-compile-probe-failed--cause-is-in-configlog", False,
     _rx(r"C compiler cannot create executables",
         r"could not compile test program either way"),
     "Read config.log in the retained chroot (haikuporter keeps work-*/ for inspection) "
     "-- configure printed a verdict, not a cause. 'C compiler cannot create "
     "executables' points at the chroot toolchain/default link line; 'could not compile "
     "test program either way' is a feature probe failing both ways. Environment, not "
     "a recipe template."),

    # 33 -- a symbol Haiku's libroot does not provide, discovered at link time (#488).
    # kbuild's `WCOREDUMP` is the shape: a POSIX-adjacent macro/symbol the source assumes
    # exists. THE MOST GENERIC LINK SIGNATURE IN THE RULESET, so it is placed nearly last
    # of the build classes, on purpose and in both directions:
    #   * after every class that names a reason -- class 18 (missing -l), class 16
    #     (duplicate symbol), class 32 (Linux-only affinity API, which surfaces through
    #     this very wording for cpuid), class 7 (LTO) -- so `undefined reference` can only
    #     claim a log that no more specific class explains;
    #   * still BEFORE classes 25/26/20, so a genuine link failure is never re-filed as a
    #     dependency-resolution problem. That mistake is exactly what #486 found.
    # auto=False: providing or patching around a missing platform symbol is porting work.
    (33, "class-33-undefined-reference-to-a-symbol-haiku-does-not-provide", False,
     _rx(r"undefined reference to "),
     "Porting work: implement/shim the missing symbol under __HAIKU__, or patch the "
     "source to a supported equivalent (kbuild's WCOREDUMP). Check first that it is not "
     "really a missing -l (class 18) or a Linux-only API with a name (class 32)."),

    # 25 -- the wave driver's own round cap, NOT a port defect. MUST be ordered before
    # class 20: a capped run ends at haikuporter's resolve step, so its log carries
    # class 20's "could not be resolved" wording and class 20 was stealing all 22 of
    # these -- which is how the census came to overstate "dependency not published"
    # (#486). Measured: the 22 ports logging `gave up after 10 attempts` were EXACTLY
    # the 22 class-20 items matched via the build-requires regex (intersection 22,
    # symmetric difference 0). auto=False: nothing to fix in the recipe -- the driver
    # cap was raised in haiku-nativebuild, so these need only a re-wave.
    (25, "class-25-wave-driver-round-cap--not-a-port-defect", False,
     _rx(r"gave up after \d+ attempts"),
     "NOT a port defect and NOT a missing dependency: haiku-nativebuild ran out of "
     "dependency-install rounds while still making progress (one requirement is "
     "cleared per round, so MAX_ATTEMPTS bounded dependency COUNT). The cap now "
     "defaults to 60 -- just re-wave the port."),

    # 26 -- the wave driver observed no progress and pkgman's own transcripts say the
    # cause is NOT absence. Also ordered before class 20 for the same reason: these
    # logs carry haikuporter's "could not be resolved" wording, and filing them as
    # "dependency not published" is precisely the guess #485 removed. The four
    # sub-verdicts (ALREADY_PRESENT / CONSTRAINT_UNSATISFIED / PKGMAN_ERROR /
    # NO_MATCH_BUT_CANDIDATES_EXIST) are printed per requirement in the log, with
    # pkgman quoted verbatim -- read them rather than re-deriving a cause here.
    # auto=False: each sub-verdict needs a different action (re-order the wave, bump a
    # version constraint, disambiguate a multi-provider name).
    (26, "class-26-dependency-present-but-unresolved--not-absent", False,
     _rx(r"DEP_NO_PROGRESS"),
     "NOT absence: pkgman reported the dependency as already installed, or "
     "version-constrained, or ambiguous, or failed for its own stated reason. The "
     "per-requirement pkgman evidence is in the log -- act on that verdict "
     "(sequencing / constraint / ambiguity), do not re-publish blindly."),

    # 20 -- prerequisite package/tool not published. The dependency-not-published
    # root cause under haikuporter's NATIVE wording ("unable to resolve
    # (pre)required packages ... could not be resolved") rather than the literal
    # UNRESOLVABLE token -- which is exactly why these evaded has_unresolvable and
    # landed in UNMATCHED (the single biggest UNMATCHED leak, ~21 ports). Also the
    # pkg-config "Package 'X', required by 'Y', not found" and libgcrypt-config
    # sub-signatures. Placed LATE (after every compile/link class) so a real build
    # error always wins first-match; only a log whose SOLE error is the resolve/
    # pkg-config/libgcrypt line lands here. auto=False: NOT a recipe edit -- build
    # and PUBLISH the dependency (see build_state=built != published).
    (20, "class-20-prerequisite-packagetool-not-published", False,
     _rx(r"unable to resolve (?:pre)?required packages",
         # the literal marker haiku-nativebuild echoes when a build-req is not in
         # the repo pool -- placed here (late) so any real earlier-class build error
         # still wins first-match. NOTE (#485): before that fix this marker was a
         # GUESS -- the driver printed it whenever the unresolved set stopped
         # shrinking, without ever reading pkgman's exit status or stderr, and 149 of
         # the 350 ports filed here named a dependency that was in the pool. The
         # driver now only prints UNRESOLVABLE when pkgman itself reported no match
         # AND a case-insensitive search of every configured repository offered no
         # candidate; every other no-progress outcome prints DEP_NO_PROGRESS and lands
         # in class 26 above. So this signature is trustworthy for logs produced AFTER
         # that fix, and still over-collects for logs produced before it.
         r"UNRESOLVABLE",
         r'build-(?:pre)?requires "[^"]+" of package .* could not be resolved',
         r"Package '[^']+', required by '[^']+', not found",
         r"libgcrypt (?:not found|is too old)",
         r"libgcrypt [0-9].* or newer is required",
         r"checking for libgcrypt-config\.\.\. no"),
     "NOT a recipe fix: build AND PUBLISH the missing dependency to green, then "
     "re-wave the consumer (or provision a missing host tool). See Class 24 for a "
     "host-only fetch tool, and the deferred-hard list for dropped libgcrypt-config."),

    # 12 -- source availability / checksum drift. NOT auto-recoverable: a bump is
    # only safe after the integrity gate, and the egress-blocked subset is not
    # recipe-fixable at all.
    (12, "class-12-source-availability--checksum-drift", False,
     _rx(r"CHECKSUM_SHA256",
         r"checksum mismatch",
         r"Expected:\s*CHECKSUM",
         r"Expected SHA-256:",          # haikuporter's own checksum-mismatch phrasing
         r"Found SHA-256:",
         r"Just a moment",              # Cloudflare challenge
         r"HTTP (?:300|403|404)\b",
         r"Multiple Choices",
         r"Download of .* failed",
         r"error downloading",
         r"Checksum error",
         # source proxy / wget-shim fetch failures (mirror down, truncated, egress):
         r"Failed to fetch source",
         r"Unable to fetch source",
         r"upstream fetch failed",
         r"\[srccache\] FAIL"),
     "MIXED / needs per-port judgement: benign recompression or a removed version "
     "is recoverable ONLY after fetch+sha256+extract+version-verify (the integrity "
     "rule); the egress-blocked subset (Cloudflare/unreachable) is not recipe-fixable."),

    # 9 -- the empty-package trap. Largely undetectable from a *failure* log (the
    # build exits 0, so such items are build_state=built, not failed). Kept as a
    # weak last-resort heuristic; expected near-zero hits in this backlog.
    (9, "class-9-the-empty-package-trap", True,
     _rx(r"Nothing to be done for 'install'",
         r"No rule to make target 'install'"),
     "Add an explicit INSTALL() that installs the built artifact. NOTE: a true "
     "empty-package build exits 0 and is build_state=built, so it rarely appears "
     "in this (failed/needs_human) backlog."),
]


# The dep-not-published marker, in BOTH forms: the literal UNRESOLVABLE token the
# wave tooling writes, AND haikuporter's own native wording (which carries no such
# token). Used for the census "of which N mention UNRESOLVABLE" and for the miner's
# exclusion parity -- the native wording is the biggest UNMATCHED leak (Class 20).
_UNRESOLVABLE = re.compile(
    r"UNRESOLVABLE|unable to resolve (?:pre)?required packages|"
    r'build-(?:pre)?requires "[^"]+" of package .* could not be resolved')


def classify(text):
    """Return (class_no|None, slug|None, auto_recoverable, fix_ref, note, matched_pat).
    None class_no means UNMATCHED. First matching rule wins."""
    for cls, slug, auto, pats, note in RULES:
        for p in pats:
            if p.search(text):
                return cls, slug, auto, anchor(slug), note, p.pattern
    return None, None, False, "", "", None


# --------------------------------------------- was a diagnostic captured at all? (#488)
# NOT every UNMATCHED log is a ruleset gap. Before 3371e78289 (2026-09-17) the failure
# path captured only `tail -20 "$log"`, and ~11 of those ~22 lines are haikuporter's
# fixed epilogue -- so for most of the pre-fix backlog the window ends before the line
# that says WHY. Those logs are unclassifiable by construction: no regex can ever read a
# line that was never captured, and counting them as "no class signature" both inflates
# UNMATCHED and libels the ruleset. They need a re-wave with the current deep capture.
#
# A line STATES A REASON if it carries a compiler/configure/linker diagnostic ...
_REASON_LINE = re.compile(r"\b(?:error|fatal error):|\bError:\s|No such file or directory|"
                          r"undefined reference|Permission denied|No space left")
# ... unless it only says that something FAILED, never why. Two of these arms carry the
# whole split and are mutation-tested in the selftest:
#   * `Error: Build has failed - stopping.` is the last line of haikuporter's epilogue, so
#     it is in EVERY log -- without this arm nothing is ever no-diagnostic;
#   * `collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status` is the usual last line of a truncated
#     link failure and says only that ld gave up.
# The make/gmake arm is different: measured over the 639-log backlog cache it changes
# nothing, because no _REASON_LINE token matches make's `*** [target] Error N` recursion
# in the first place. It is kept, and labelled inert, because those lines are the
# canonical contentless ending of a truncated window and the first _REASON_LINE token
# that reaches them would otherwise silently un-split the census.
_CONTENTLESS_LINE = re.compile(
    r"^(?:make|gmake)(?:\[\d+\])?: \*\*\*|"          # inert today, see above
    r"^collect2: error: ld returned|"                 # load-bearing
    r"^Error: Build has failed|"                      # load-bearing (epilogue, every log)
    r"^\S+: FAILED, no missing-dep|"
    r"^Warning: Command ")


def has_captured_diagnostic(text):
    """True when at least one line in the captured window states a REASON for the
    failure. False means the window holds only boilerplate, warnings and exit-status
    recursion -- an evidence-capture artifact, not a missing class signature."""
    for line in text.splitlines():
        s = line.strip()
        if _CONTENTLESS_LINE.match(s):
            continue
        if _REASON_LINE.search(s):
            return True
    return False


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- DDB reading
def load_items(table, region):
    """Read every failed + needs_human item off the by-build-state GSI. Returns a
    list of dicts: {pkg, build_state, log_url, error_class, target}. Deterministic
    field extraction only (no free-text judgement)."""
    import boto3
    ddb = boto3.client("dynamodb", region_name=region)
    paginator = ddb.get_paginator("query")
    out = []
    for state in STATES:
        for page in paginator.paginate(
                TableName=table, IndexName=GSI,
                KeyConditionExpression="build_state = :s",
                ExpressionAttributeValues={":s": {"S": state}},
                ProjectionExpression="pkg, build_state, log_url, last_error_class, "
                                     "target_version"):
            for it in page.get("Items", []):
                out.append({
                    "pkg": it["pkg"]["S"],
                    "build_state": it["build_state"]["S"],
                    "log_url": it.get("log_url", {}).get("S") or "",
                    "error_class": it.get("last_error_class", {}).get("S") or "",
                    "target": it.get("target_version", {}).get("S") or "",
                })
    return out


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- S3 fetching
_S3_URI = re.compile(r"^s3://([^/]+)/(.+)$")


def fetch_log(s3, log_url):
    """Fetch + gunzip a build log. Returns (text|None, reason). None text means the
    log is missing/empty/unreadable -> NO_LOG, with a short reason string."""
    if not log_url or log_url == "n/a":
        return None, "no log_url"
    m = _S3_URI.match(log_url)
    if not m:
        return None, "bad log_url: %s" % log_url
    bucket, key = m.group(1), m.group(2)
    try:
        obj = s3.get_object(Bucket=bucket, Key=key)
        raw = obj["Body"].read()
    except Exception as e:  # NoSuchKey, AccessDenied, etc. -- treat as NO_LOG
        return None, "%s" % type(e).__name__
    if not raw:
        return None, "empty object"
    # Logs are gzip (run_ssm._wrapper: `gzip -9 -c`), but the wrapper falls back to
    # `cp` (plain) if gzip is unavailable -- so tolerate both.
    if raw[:2] == b"\x1f\x8b":
        try:
            data = gzip.GzipFile(fileobj=io.BytesIO(raw)).read()
        except OSError:
            data = raw
    else:
        data = raw
    text = data.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    if not text.strip():
        return None, "empty after decode"
    return text, "ok"


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- DDB writing
def write_triage(table, region, pkg, cls, auto, fix_ref, ts):
    import boto3
    ddb = boto3.resource("dynamodb", region_name=region).Table(table)
    triage_class = str(cls) if isinstance(cls, int) else cls  # class no | NO_LOG | UNMATCHED
    ddb.update_item(
        Key={"pkg": pkg},
        UpdateExpression=("SET triage_class=:c, triage_auto_recoverable=:a, "
                          "triage_fix_ref=:f, triage_ts=:t"),
        ExpressionAttributeValues={":c": triage_class, ":a": bool(auto),
                                   ":f": fix_ref or "n/a", ":t": ts},
    )


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- main
def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Triage the DeBeOS build-wave failure backlog against the arm64 "
                    "porting playbook (#90/#136). Dry-run by default.")
    ap.add_argument("--table", default=TABLE_DEFAULT)
    ap.add_argument("--region", default=os.environ.get("AWS_DEFAULT_REGION", "us-west-2"))
    ap.add_argument("--limit", type=int, default=0,
                    help="classify only the first N items (smoke test). 0 = all.")
    ap.add_argument("--workers", type=int, default=16,
                    help="parallel S3 log fetches (read-only). Default 16.")
    ap.add_argument("--out", help="write the full per-port classification to this JSON path.")
    ap.add_argument("--csv", help="write the per-port classification to this CSV path.")
    ap.add_argument("--dump-unmatched", help="write each UNMATCHED port's log tail to "
                    "this dir (for extending the ruleset).")
    ap.add_argument("--show-matches", action="store_true",
                    help="print, per port, the class and the regex that matched.")
    ap.add_argument("--execute", action="store_true",
                    help="ACTUALLY write triage_* back to DDB (additive attributes only). "
                         "Default is DRY-RUN: classify + census, write nothing.")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    try:
        import boto3  # noqa: F401
    except ImportError:
        raise SystemExit("boto3 is required (pip install boto3).")

    log("=== DeBeOS failure-backlog triage ===")
    log("mode              : %s" % ("EXECUTE (writes triage_* to DDB)" if args.execute
                                    else "DRY-RUN (classify + census, writes nothing)"))
    log("table / index     : %s / %s  (states: %s)" % (args.table, GSI, ", ".join(STATES)))

    try:
        items = load_items(args.table, args.region)
    except Exception as e:
        raise SystemExit("FATAL: could not scan DDB %s.%s: %s -- refusing to invent a "
                         "census." % (args.table, GSI, e))
    if not items:
        raise SystemExit("FATAL: 0 items with build_state in %s -- the GSI/table name "
                         "may be wrong. Refusing to invent a census." % (STATES,))

    if args.limit:
        items = items[:args.limit]
    log("backlog to triage : %d items" % len(items))

    import boto3
    s3 = boto3.client("s3", region_name=args.region)

    # ---- fetch + classify (parallel S3 reads) --------------------------------
    results = {}  # pkg -> record

    def work(it):
        text, reason = fetch_log(s3, it["log_url"])
        if text is None:
            rec = dict(it, triage_class="NO_LOG", auto=False, fix_ref="",
                       note="log missing/empty (%s)" % reason, matched=None,
                       has_unresolvable=False, no_diagnostic=False)
        else:
            cls, slug, auto, fix_ref, note, matched = classify(text)
            # only meaningful for UNMATCHED: a classified log obviously had a diagnostic.
            nodiag = cls is None and not has_captured_diagnostic(text)
            if nodiag:
                note = ("no diagnostic line in the captured window (pre-3371e78289 "
                        "tail -20 artifact) -- NOT a ruleset gap; re-wave with the "
                        "current deep capture. #488")
            rec = dict(it,
                       triage_class=(cls if cls is not None else "UNMATCHED"),
                       auto=auto, fix_ref=fix_ref, note=note, matched=matched,
                       has_unresolvable=bool(_UNRESOLVABLE.search(text)),
                       no_diagnostic=nodiag,
                       _text_tail=text[-4000:] if cls is None else None)
        return it["pkg"], rec

    with cf.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=max(1, args.workers)) as ex:
        for pkg, rec in ex.map(work, items):
            results[pkg] = rec
            if args.show_matches:
                log("  %-28s state=%-11s class=%-9s auto=%-5s %s" % (
                    pkg, rec["build_state"], rec["triage_class"], rec["auto"],
                    ("/%s/" % rec["matched"]) if rec["matched"] else ""))

    # ---- dump unmatched for ruleset extension --------------------------------
    if args.dump_unmatched:
        # Split the dump the same way the census does (#488): the top level holds the
        # logs that DO state a reason -- the real ruleset-extension frontier -- and
        # no-diagnostic/ holds the truncated-window logs, which no regex can help.
        # Mixing them buries the 20-odd actionable ones under a hundred that are not.
        nodiag_dir = os.path.join(args.dump_unmatched, "no-diagnostic")
        os.makedirs(args.dump_unmatched, exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(nodiag_dir, exist_ok=True)
        n = nd = 0
        for pkg, rec in results.items():
            if rec["triage_class"] == "UNMATCHED" and rec.get("_text_tail"):
                d = nodiag_dir if rec["no_diagnostic"] else args.dump_unmatched
                with open(os.path.join(d, "%s.tail.log" % pkg), "w") as f:
                    f.write(rec["_text_tail"])
                if rec["no_diagnostic"]:
                    nd += 1
                else:
                    n += 1
        log("dumped %d UNMATCHED log tails WITH a diagnostic to %s, and %d with none to "
            "%s" % (n, args.dump_unmatched, nd, nodiag_dir))

    # ---- write-back ----------------------------------------------------------
    ts = int(time.time())
    if args.execute:
        wrote = 0
        for pkg, rec in results.items():
            write_triage(args.table, args.region, pkg, rec["triage_class"],
                         rec["auto"], rec["fix_ref"], ts)
            wrote += 1
        log("wrote triage_* to %d items (additive attrs only; build_state untouched)." % wrote)
    else:
        log("(dry-run) would write triage_* to %d items; re-run with --execute." % len(results))

    # ---- artifacts -----------------------------------------------------------
    rows = []
    for pkg, rec in sorted(results.items()):
        rows.append({"pkg": pkg, "build_state": rec["build_state"],
                     "triage_class": rec["triage_class"],
                     "auto_recoverable": rec["auto"],
                     "fix_ref": rec["fix_ref"],
                     "error_class": rec["error_class"],
                     "has_unresolvable": rec["has_unresolvable"],
                     "no_diagnostic_captured": rec["no_diagnostic"],
                     "note": rec["note"]})
    if args.out:
        with open(args.out, "w") as f:
            json.dump({"generated_at": ts, "table": args.table, "count": len(rows),
                       "ports": rows}, f, indent=2)
        log("wrote JSON classification: %s" % args.out)
    if args.csv:
        with open(args.csv, "w", newline="") as f:
            w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=list(rows[0].keys()))
            w.writeheader()
            w.writerows(rows)
        log("wrote CSV classification: %s" % args.csv)

    # ---- census (the payoff) -------------------------------------------------
    by_class = Counter(str(rec["triage_class"]) for rec in results.values())
    auto_ct = sum(1 for rec in results.values() if rec["auto"])
    matched_hard = sum(1 for rec in results.values()
                       if isinstance(rec["triage_class"], int) and not rec["auto"])
    unmatched = by_class.get("UNMATCHED", 0)
    no_log = by_class.get("NO_LOG", 0)
    unmatched_unres = sum(1 for rec in results.values()
                          if rec["triage_class"] == "UNMATCHED" and rec["has_unresolvable"])
    # #488: split UNMATCHED into the part the ruleset could still claim and the part no
    # ruleset can ever claim, so classifier coverage is not judged on truncated logs.
    nodiag = sum(1 for rec in results.values() if rec["no_diagnostic"])
    ruleset_gap = unmatched - nodiag

    # class metadata for the report
    meta = {c: (slug, auto) for c, slug, auto, _, _ in RULES}
    class_nums = sorted(meta)  # all classes the ruleset knows (1-14 + 18-26)

    print("\n" + "=" * 82)
    print("DeBeOS failure-backlog census  (%d items: %s)" %
          (len(results), " + ".join("%d %s" % (
              sum(1 for r in results.values() if r["build_state"] == s), s)
              for s in STATES)))
    print("=" * 82)
    print("%-6s %-62s %5s %s" % ("class", "playbook section", "count", "auto"))
    print("-" * 82)
    for c in class_nums:
        cnt = by_class.get(str(c), 0)
        if not cnt:
            continue
        slug, auto = meta[c]
        print("%-6d %-62s %5d %s" % (c, slug, cnt, "yes" if auto else "NO"))
    if unmatched:
        print("%-6s %-62s %5d %s" % ("UNMATCH", "  ruleset gap (a diagnostic IS captured)",
                                     ruleset_gap, "NO"))
        print("%-6s %-62s %5d %s" % ("", "  NO_DIAGNOSTIC captured (truncated window)",
                                     nodiag, "NO"))
    if by_class.get("NO_LOG"):
        print("%-6s %-62s %5d %s" % ("NO_LOG", "-", by_class["NO_LOG"], "NO"))
    print("-" * 82)
    print("AUTO-RECOVERABLE (known playbook template) : %4d" % auto_ct)
    print("GENUINELY-HARD  (matched a class, auto=NO) : %4d" % matched_hard)
    print("UNMATCHED (no class signature)             : %4d  (of which %d mention "
          "UNRESOLVABLE = dep unbuilt/unpublished)" % (unmatched, unmatched_unres))
    # The split that keeps the coverage number honest (#488). A no-diagnostic log is an
    # evidence-capture artifact from the pre-3371e78289 `tail -20`: the window ends
    # before the line that says why, so it is unclassifiable by construction and must
    # not be read as a missing class signature.
    print("  |- ruleset gap: a reason line IS captured : %4d  <- extend RULES here"
          % ruleset_gap)
    print("  |- NO DIAGNOSTIC in the captured window   : %4d  <- no regex can ever "
          "classify these; re-wave with the current deep capture (#488)" % nodiag)
    print("NO_LOG (log missing/empty)                 : %4d" % no_log)
    print("=" * 82)

    # top auto-recoverable classes by port count -> next re-wave targets
    auto_classes = sorted(
        ((by_class.get(str(c), 0), c, meta[c][0]) for c in class_nums if meta[c][1]),
        reverse=True)
    top = [(c, slug, n) for (n, c, slug) in auto_classes if n][:3]
    if top:
        print("Top auto-recoverable classes (next re-wave targets):")
        for c, slug, n in top:
            print("   class %-2d  %-62s %4d ports" % (c, slug, n))
        print("=" * 82)

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
